window.VD=window.VD||{};window.VD["vw/eos-1f"]={"eos-1f-facelift-activate-ambient-lighting":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate ambient lighting. (After wiring and lighting installation for retrofitting)   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 Ambient lighting activation. 1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket:</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>20</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Footwell Lights installed”.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2 Ambient lighting setting :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Channel <strong>27</strong> “Footwell Lighting Dimming (with open Door)”.</p>\n<p><em>Set a value between 0 and 200 (for 100% lighting, set 200).</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 3 Activate ambient lighting while driving :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>12</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>5</strong>: Check “Footwell Lights withbTerminal 58d active”.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 4 Ambient lighting control while driving :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> Channel<strong> 35</strong> “Footwell Lighting Dimming (with Terminal 58d)”.</p>\n<p><em>Set a value between 0 and 200 (for 100% lighting, set 200).</em></p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-bluetooth-on-rns-315":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to activate Bluetooth on the RNS 315.  </p>\n<p><strong>ATTENTION: Not all RNS-315 are equipped with a Bluetooth module.</strong> </p>\n<figure><table><thead><tr><td>Module compatibility list</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3C0 035 279</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 A</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 B</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 C</td><td>Yes (conditional)</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 D</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 E</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 F</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 G</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 H</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 J</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>3C0 035 279 K</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>You can also remove the RNS 315 from its slot to check. Behind it, you’ll find a label with the RNS-315’s MAC address.<br/>If the MAC address contains only zeros (MAC: 00:00:00:00:00:00), your RNS-315 is not equipped with the Bluetooth module. </p>\n<figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns_315_j_mac_non-6328b9e5.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns315_mac_oui-eed1a386.webp\"/></figure>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>Select</strong>”, then click on “<strong>Select</strong>”. <strong>Procedure:</strong> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket. <strong><strong>“37 – Navigation</strong></strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>3</strong><br/>Bit <strong>5</strong>: Uncheck the “Telephone Transceiver inactive” box</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/RNS315_3C0-035-279_Module37_Byte3-74e93c9c.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns-315-bt-8b82eb41.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-coming-leaving-home":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to activate Coming Home and Leaving Home. And add it to the ODB (MFA)   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1 <em>(AFTER the end of 2009)</em>:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>12</strong>:<br/>Disable bit <strong>2</strong><em>“Coming-Home Mode (0 = Automatic / 1 = Manual)</em>”<br/>Set bit <strong>2</strong> for display in ODB (MFA)</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cominghomeg6-f70c52d7.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>Byte <strong>17</strong>:</p>\n<p>Bit <strong>5</strong>: Check “Coming-Home (CH) active”<br/>Bit <strong>6</strong>: Check “Leaving-Home (CH) active”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cominghomeg62-efef7c12.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cominghomeg63-3c107aa3.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cominghomeg64-189ea28a.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/cominghomeg65-b5621af6.webp\"/></figure>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>Line 1 <em>(BEFORE the end of 2009</em>):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>23</strong>:<br/>Disable bit <strong>5</strong></p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-daytime-running-lights-drl-only-with-headlight-switch-in-auto-position":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate daytime running lights (DRL) only when the light <strong>switch</strong> is in Auto position.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>26</strong>: <br/>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Check “Daytime Running Light (DRL) only active with Assistance Driving Light (ADL) active”.  </p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-driving-school-mode-rcd-210-rcd-310":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to add a Driving School mode view to the RCD 210, RCD 310 that displays turn signals, speed … (Also ideal for accompanied driving) <br/><br/><br/><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket. <br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“56 – Radio</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Channel 2</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – On this same page, there is a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>On this menu, select<strong>“1</strong>” and confirm.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/golf6_golf_vi_fahrschulfunktion-5b883ac1.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-headlamp-washers":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the headlamp washers.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>20</strong>: Bit<strong> 5</strong>: Check “Headlight Washer Installed”.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-hidden-menu-rns-315":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to activate the hidden menu in RNS 315.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>Select</strong>”, then click on “<strong>Select</strong>”. <strong>Procedure:</strong> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket. <strong><strong>“37 – Navigation</strong></strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <em><strong>Developer mode (temporary)</strong> </em>then set the value to <strong><em>Enabled</em></strong></p>\n<p><em>Restart RNS 315.</em></p>\n<p><em>To launch the Hidden Menu, press SETUP (for 6s)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/3C0-035-279_Module37_Adaptation_Developer_mode_tem-59109da4.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns-315-bt-8b82eb41.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-hidden-menu-rns-510":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Activates the hidden menu in RNS 510.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>Select</strong> ” and run the VCDS program.<strong><strong>56 – Radio</strong></strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Choose <em><strong>Channel 50</strong> </em>then set the value to <strong>1</strong></p>\n<p><em>Restart the RNS 510.</em></p>\n<p><em>To launch the Hidden Menu, press SETUP (for 6s)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/1T0-035-680-C_Module37_Adaptation_Channel50-1a7d6a90.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns510-1024x821-d0438887.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-multifunction-steering-wheel-and-paddle-shifter-retrofit":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: activates multifunction steering wheel with/without paddles (retrofit)  </p>\n<p><strong>(Modifications only possible after retrofitting the multifunction steering wheel)  </strong></p>\n<p><em>Be careful how you attach your old airbag, if you want to reuse it with the new steering wheel. There are 2 types of attachment.<br/>Check your steering control module to see if it’s compatible with the steering wheel controls. Here’s a page on the Ross-Tech website <a href=\"http://wiki.ross-tech.com/wiki/index.php/Steering_Wheel_Control_Module_Versions\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">with all the Golf 6 module versions</a>. Make sure you choose a compatible module (HighLine, with Slip Ring and Steering Angle Sensor identical to your old module).  </em> </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/></p>\n<p><strong>Line 1 (For modules 1K0-953-549…):</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>16 – Multifunction Steering Wheel.”</strong><br/><br/><strong> </strong>4<strong>– </strong>Click <strong>“Coding – 07”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – <em>Set the value according to the desired options (replace the ? with the corresponding number)</em> </p>\n<p><em><strong>0xxx5xx</strong>: Multi-function steering wheel, without paddles, without voice commands and deactivation of ODB control with windshield wiper comodo</em><br/><em><strong>0xxx6xx</strong>: Multi-function steering wheel, with paddles, without voice commands and deactivation of ODB control with windshield wiper co-modo</em><br/><em><strong>0xxx7xx</strong>: Multi-function steering wheel, without paddles, with voice commands and ODB control deactivation with windshield wiper comodo</em><br/><em><strong>0xxx8xx</strong>: Multi-function steering wheel, with paddles, voice commands and ODB control deactivation with windshield wiper comodo</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/regulateur3-945b80f3.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>After changing the module, you should see the ESP and power steering lights on the ODB.</p>\n<p><strong>Angle sensor recalibration</strong></p>\n<p>Prerequisites.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start the vehicle</li>\n<li>turn the steering wheel all the way to the right, then all the way to the left</li>\n<li>Move the vehicle forward a few metres, but do not exceed 20 km/h.</li>\n<li>If the steering is hard during this test, stop the vehicle with the wheels aligned and the steering wheel straight.</li>\n<li>Don’t move the steering wheel.</li>\n<li>Leave the engine running and do not switch off the ignition.</li>\n<li>The power supply must be at least 12V.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>[44 – Power steering] -&gt; [Coding II – 11] (40168) -&gt; [Basic setting – 04].</p>\n<p><em>Select group <strong>060</strong> and press <strong>ON/OFF/NEXT</strong>. The first field should then display <strong>OK</strong> </em></p>\n<p><strong>Check</strong></p>\n<p>[44 – Power Steering] -&gt; [Measurement groups – 08]</p>\n<p><em>Select group <strong>007</strong> and press <strong>Go!</strong> The first field must have a value between <strong>-1.5° and 1.5°</strong>. </em></p>\n<p><strong>Power steering adaptation</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Engine still running</li>\n<li>Center the steering wheel and keep it centered for 5s</li>\n<li>Turn the steering wheel all the way to the left and hold it there for 10s.</li>\n<li>Turn the steering wheel all the way to the right and hold it there for 10s.</li>\n<li>Center the steering wheel</li>\n<li>Turn off the engine and switch off the ignition</li>\n<li>Switch the ignition back on.</li>\n<li>Wait 10s.</li>\n<li>The light on the ODB should go out. If not, drive a short distance. </li>\n</ul>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>Paddle shift activation</strong> <strong>for modules 5K0-953-5… …</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click <strong>“16 – Multifunction Steering Wheel.”</strong><br/><br/><strong>4 – </strong>Click “ <strong>Coding – 07”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – <em>Byte <strong>01</strong></em></p>\n<p>Set bit <strong>04</strong></p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-reversing-camera-retrofit":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables the rearview camera after it has been installed on the RCD 510/330/360/440 and RNS 315/510 (retrofit).  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>37 – Navigation</strong> ” (RNS) or “<strong>56 – Radio</strong>” (RCD)<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>5 – </strong> Select byte <strong>3</strong><br/>. Then set bit <strong>6</strong> to “Rear View Camera (RVC) installed/active.”</p>\n<p><strong><strong><strong>Line 2 (Optional, if parking assist is available)</strong> <strong>:</strong></strong></strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>10 – Parking Assistance</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>5 – </strong> Select byte <strong>0</strong><br/>. Then set bit <strong>5</strong> to “Rear View Camera installed.”</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/CA86A611-2F74-4201-B791-7A74BC107A01-1024x482-c9f00e16.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><br/><strong>Hardware upgrade :</strong></p>\n<p>The camera’s video cable is connected to the infotainment control unit. It is installed in the glovebox.   <br/><br/>The two pins from the backup camera are used in the 12-pin Quadlock connector (blue).<br/><br/>Quadblock Assignment :<br/><br/>Pin 6 = video line<br/>Pin 12 = shielding</p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-rns-510-retrofit":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To enable installation of the RNS 510 to replace the original RCD-310 (retrofit).  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 (RNS-510 Activation in the Gateway):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“19-Gateway CAN</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Installation List</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – <em>Check “37- Navigation</em></p>\n<p><em>Click on “Save coding</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2 (Media-in activation</strong>)<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte <strong>0</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>2</strong>: Check “AUX-Input Connection Monitoring active”.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 3 (RNS-510 without Dynaudio</strong>)<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 1</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>0-7 </strong>: Select “00 Sound System not installed”.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 4 (Original speakers</strong>)<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 2</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>0-7</strong>: Select “04 4 Speaker Channels (Front and Rear)”.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 5 (RNS-510 theme</strong>)<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Encoding 07</strong>.” Then click ” <strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>.” </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 7</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bits <strong>4-5</strong>: Select one of the 3 themes</em><br/><em>“00 Display Variant: Highline”</em><br/><em>“10 Display Variant: Sportsline”</em><br/><em>“20 Display Variant: Premium”</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/2B758E5F-ACB4-418C-BBBB-B61B0F3A298B-186113f1.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 6 (Display of navigation information on the ODB</strong>)<strong>:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 7</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>7</strong>: Check “Communication Instrument Cluster via Operation and Display Protocol (BAP)”.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 6 (Vehicle selection displayed when OPS is activated) :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 9</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>0-7</strong>: Select “05 Optical Park System &amp; Interior Graphics: VW Golf (5K1)”.</em></p>\n<p>Exit the module and save your changes. The RNS-510 must now be restarted for the changes to take effect. Press both arrows and the star for 6s.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns510-1024x821-d0438887.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-rns-510-voice-command":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to activate voice control on the RNS 510 (SDS).  </p>\n<p><strong>ATTENTION:</strong> RNS 510 firmware must be version v2660.</p>\n<p>Activating voice control brings up the “Voice control” menu in the RNS 510’s SETUP menu.</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>Select</strong>”, then click on “<strong>Select</strong>”. <strong>Procedure:</strong> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket. <strong><strong>“37 – Navigation</strong></strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>3</strong>, check the box for<strong>“Bit 1: Microphone Diagnostics active</strong>”.</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>7</strong>, uncheck the box for<strong>“Bit 1: Voice Control inactive</strong>”.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/RNS510_Menu_Commande_Vocale-bf5955d7.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>To install the microphone, connect the microphone lead to the rear of the RNS 510. Connect to pins (Mic-)C1 and (Mic+)C7. </p>","eos-1f-facelift-adapting-brake-assist":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to configure brake assist.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“03 – ABS</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select Channel <strong>09</strong> (Brake Assist from the drop-down list)</p>\n<p><em>Choose a value</em><br/> 0<em>“medium” </em><br/>1 “low” (with low assistance, the brake pedal will be harder and more pressure will be required to brake)<br/>2 “high” (with high assistance, the brake pedal will be softer and less pressure will be required to brake)</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/assistfreing6-05b83882.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-add-daytime-running-lights-drl-to-odb":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to enable or disable daytime running lights (DRL) in the ODB (MFA). (2011+)</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>15</strong>: Bit <strong>7</strong>: Check “Daytime Running Light (DRL) Active Adaptation (MFA+)”</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/drlmfag6-54cdc95d.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/drlmfag62-c1d9cfb9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-add-mirror-adjustment-to-odb":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to add the mirror adjustment menu, synchronize or individual in the ODB (MFA). (2011+)</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>08</strong>: <em>Check Bit <strong>6</strong> </em><em>“Synchronized Mirror Adjustment</em>”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/mirrorg6-b05eff1b.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/mirrorg62-c24fc65a.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/mirrorg63-c1fe0276.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-adjust-light-sensor-sensitivity":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>Adjusts the sensitivity of the light sensor.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “2 EPIRBs” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>0</strong>:  </em><br/><em>Bits <strong>3–4</strong>: Select from the drop-down list<br/>– 00 Normal light activation threshold<br/>– 08 Earlier light activation threshold (+20%)<br/>– 10 Light Activation Threshold earlier (+40%)<br/>– 08 Light Activation Threshold earlier (+60%)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Calc09-RLS-140kmhBit0-Octet0-4f74e4e9.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Or if you have an older model</strong>:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>2</strong>:<br/><strong>Bits 0–7</strong>: Light Sensor Correction</p>","eos-1f-facelift-adjusting-the-volume-of-the-reversing-beep":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to adjust the volume of the reversing beep.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“10-Station assistance</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>6 – </strong>Select Channel: <em>“rear warning buzzer volume”. </em> </p>\n<p>Adjust value accordingly<br/>(default: 5)</p>","eos-1f-facelift-automatic-closing-of-windows-and-roof-in-case-of-rain":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Automatically closes the windows and sunroof when it rains. </p>\n<p>The vehicle must be locked using the remote control for this to work. </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/>3 – Click on ” <strong>Select</strong> “, then click on ” <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” . Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program. 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vitregolf561-c90da3e5.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select module “2” and click “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vitregolf56-9439cbb9.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – In the window that appears, select byte<strong> 0</strong> and check bit <strong>2</strong>, <strong>“Rain Closing active</strong>,” to enable the windows and roof to close when it rains:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Vag-Com-Fermeture-Automatique-des-vitres-5-d61c5dd9.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>7 – After completing this step, click on the “Exit” menu</p>\n<p> Then click “OK”</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Vag-Com-Fermeture-Automatique-des-vitres-7-353fb01b.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2: </strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> Select <em>Byte<strong> 4</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>5</strong>: Check “Comfort Operation Windows/Sunroof via Rain Sensor (0 = nonrecurring / 1 = permanent)” </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>6: </strong> Check “Comfort Operation Windows/Sunroof via Rain Sensor (Rain Closing) active” </em> </p>","eos-1f-facelift-automatic-door-locks":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to set automatic door locking from 15km/h.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>09 – Cent. Elect</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>5 </strong>– Channel 04 (Auto-Lock drop-down list) <em>Set value to <strong>1</strong></em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2 If the adaptation channel is not enabled.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong><strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 – </strong> Click “<strong>Encoding – 07</strong>,” then “<strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong></p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>2 : </strong> Check “Auto-Lock active”. </em> </p>","eos-1f-facelift-automatic-door-unlocking":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to set automatic door unlocking when the key is removed.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> Channel 05 (drop-down list (Auto-Unlock)) <em>Set value to <strong>1</strong></em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw-door-unlock-798a1163.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2 If the adaptation channel is not enabled.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>09 – Power Plant.</strong>“</p>\n<p><strong>4 – </strong> Click “<strong>Encoding – 07</strong>,” then “<strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong></p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>0 : </strong></em><br/><em>Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Auto-Unlock active”. </em> </p>","eos-1f-facelift-automatic-headlamp-activation-in-case-of-rain":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to automatically turn on the lights when it’s raining and the wipers have been triggered.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition switched off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “2 EPIRBs” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>0</strong> Bit<strong> 1 </strong>: Check “Rain Light Function Active”.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Calc09-RLS-140kmhBit0-Octet0-4f74e4e9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-automatic-headlamps-from-140-km-h":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to automatically switch on the lights when the vehicle is travelling at over 140 km/h. </p>\n<p>(The lights will automatically go out if the vehicle is travelling at less than 60 km/h) </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “2 EPIRBs” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>0</strong>, Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Highway Light Function active above (140kph/90mph)”. </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/audi_a5_8t_stg09_auswahl-steuergerate-rls-wws-bord-c6f10e6e.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Calc09-RLS-140kmhBit0-Octet0-4f74e4e9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-automatically-folding-mirrors":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Automatically folds mirrors when vehicle is locked.  </p>\n<p>For 4th generation door modules (models from 2013) activation is possible with VCDS VAG-COM (Procedure with VCP for 3rd generation at bottom of page).</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>42 – Driver’s door</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Security Access – 16</strong>” then enter the code<strong>“04354</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> Select the Channel:<strong>“System_Parameter_0-System</strong> Parameter Byte8” in the drop-down list.<br/><br/>Replace the Value <strong>90</strong> (if several values the 90 is in the middle, example: xxxx90xxxx )</p>\n<p>Set value according to mirror opening mode: <br/> – <strong>95</strong> (mirrors<em> will deploy when door is opened)</em><br/><em> – <strong>99</strong> (mirrors will deploy when ignition is switched on)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/DCA8744A-30AB-4C05-B633-4F3B76D90C50-4f152256.webp\"/></figure>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>VCP</strong> (Vag Can Pro) : For 3rd generation door modules (models up to 2011) activation is only possible with VCP.</p>\n<p>Click on Guided Functions then select the “Convenience” module on the right then “Activation automatic folder mirrors in UDS door control units (Passat/ Tiguan / Jetta)”.</p>\n<p>Setting, Command: “Folding with driver door” or “Folding with ignition” = with driver door or with key</p>\n<p>Engine/Car type : CAN TP20 1K0/5K0</p>\n<p>Start the procedure, but don’t forget to save the Conductor 42 door module with access code 04354.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-change-the-image-of-the-rcd-rns-ops":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to change the vehicle image of the OPS (parking aid) on the RCD 510/330/360/440 and RNS 315/510.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Turn on the ignition, then launch the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>37 – Navigation</strong> ” (RNS) or “<strong>56 – Radio</strong>” (RCD)</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Encoding 07</strong>,” then ” <strong>Long Coding Helper</strong> “</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Choose Byte<strong> 6</strong> or <strong>9</strong></p>\n<p><em>Select the vehicle from the drop-down menu:</em><br/><br/>00 = via CAN<br/>01 = Polo 6R<br/>02 = New Beetle<br/>03 = New Beetle Cabrio<br/>04 = Scirocco<br/>05 = Golf 5<br/>06 = Jetta<br/>07 = Golf 6 Variant<br/>08 = Golf 6 Cabrio<br/>09 = Touran<br/>0A = Golf Plus<br/>0C = Tiguan<br/>0D = Sharan<br/>0E = EOS<br/>0F = Passat 3C<br/>10 = Passat Limousine<br/>11 = Passat Variant<br/>13 = Caddy<br/>14 = T5<br/>2C = Skoda Octavia<br/>2D = Skoda Octavia Kombi<br/>2E = Skoda Superb Kombi</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/IMG_0864-978ed9ed.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>Exit the module and save your changes. The RCD or RNS must now be restarted for the changes to take effect. Press both arrows and the star for 6s.  </p>","eos-1f-facelift-change-the-rcd-rns-510-theme":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to change the display theme of the RCD 510 and RNS 510.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>” for RNS510 and “<strong>56 – Radio</strong>” for RCD510.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>” then “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 7</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>4-5</strong>: Select from 3 themes</em><br/><em>“00 Display Variant: Highline</em><br/><em>“10 Display Variant: Sportsline</em><br/><em>“20 Display Variant: Premium</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/2B758E5F-ACB4-418C-BBBB-B61B0F3A298B-186113f1.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/69C76009-6EC7-465A-A676-8E39BA840A6B-6689b9e0.webp\"/><figcaption>Highline</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/3A58F148-9FBB-4DD8-BF05-E03A5351E3A8-e9b6cb9b.webp\"/><figcaption>Sportsline</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/4894A3B7-2ED2-4A75-9D46-CA3BFCF5A76C-8fb8de15.webp\"/><figcaption>Premium</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Exit the module and save your changes. The RCD / RNS 510 must now be restarted for the changes to take effect. Press both arrows and the star for 6s.  </p>","eos-1f-facelift-comfort-turn-signal":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set the number of flashes for the “Comfort flashing” option.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4 – </strong> Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>5 </strong>– Select<strong>“Comfort Turn Signal”</strong> from the drop-down list </p>\n<p>Choose a value between 1 and 5 for the number of flashes</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/confortturnsignalg6-6da68fad.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><em>Note: If you have a Golf 6 after 2011, and the number of flashes has not changed after coding, you may need to activate the option in the MFA+ ODB.<br/>In Options, go to Settings, then Lighting, Factory settings, then tick Factory settings and select OK.</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/golf6-turn-signal-blinks-e68241dd.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-coming-leaving-home-with-fog-lamps":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>Enables fog lamps to be used for Coming Home and Leaving Home.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte<strong> 17</strong>: <br/>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Check “Coming-Home (CH) via Fog-Light active”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5_774-e325de60.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-coming-leaving-home-with-xenons":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>Activates xenon headlamps instead of fog lamps for Coming Home and Leaving Home.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition switched off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>17 </strong>:  </p>\n<p>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Uncheck “Coming-Home (CH) via Fog-Light active”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5_774-e325de60.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-coming-home-settings":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong> Allows you to configure the Coming-Home behavior.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>The Coming-Home timer starts when the door opens<br/><em>Byte <strong>12</strong>:  </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Coming-Home Timer (0 = Start with Term.15 OFF / 1 = Start with Door Opening)”   </em></p>\n<p>The Coming-Home timer starts when the ignition is turned off<br/><em>Byte <strong>12</strong>:  </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Uncheck “Coming-Home Timer (0 = Start with Term.15 OFF / 1 = Start with Door Opening)”   </em></p>\n<p>The Coming-Home feature is activated manually by flashing the headlights<br/><em>Byte <strong>12</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>2</strong>: Uncheck “Coming-Home Mode (0 = automatic / 1 = manual)” </em> </p>\n<p>Coming-Home is activated automatically<br/><em>Byte <strong>12</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>2</strong>: Check “Coming-Home Mode (0 = automatic / 1 = manual)” </em> </p>","eos-1f-facelift-corner-lights-activation":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Activating the <strong>turn signals</strong> causes the fog light on the side toward which you’re turning the steering wheel to turn on when traveling at speeds below 50 km/h. This feature is also called “cornering lights.” Once this feature is activated, the fog light on the side of the turn you’re making will turn on! It’s super handy on narrow roads or in roundabouts.     </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostic port.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Turn on the ignition, then launch the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>09 – Power Control Unit</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/4-Activation-des-feux-en-virage-sur-Golf-5-et-6-f4e51c90.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – In the window that appears, select  </p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>12</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>4</strong>: Check “Cornering Light Activation Time Limited”</em></p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>14: </strong></em><br/><em>Bit <strong>7: </strong> Check “Cornering Lights via Front Fog Lights active”</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/5-Activation-de-la-fonction-Cornering-Lights-via-F-255e876b.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>7</strong> – After completing this step, click on the “Exit” menu<br/>, then click “OK.”</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6-Activation-de-la-fonction-Cornering-Lights-via-F-2a08cc40.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-corner-lights-in-reverse":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to activate the cornering lights with the fog lamps when reversing.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>26</strong>: Bit <strong>6</strong>: Check “Enabling Cornering Light with Reverse Gear active”.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-cruise-control-activation-retrofit":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to activate GRA cruise control (retrofit). </p>\n<p><strong><strong>Modification only possible after retrofitting cruise control switch </strong>and <a href=\"https://wiki.ross-tech.com/wiki/index.php/Steering_Wheel_Control_Module_Versions\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">check that your multifunction steering wheel is compatible (midline or highline)</a>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>01 – Motor</strong> “.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on ” <strong>Coding II (11) or Login 11</strong> “. </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/regulateur1-1c279499.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Then enter code <strong>11463</strong>, then <strong>OK</strong> (Do It!)</p>\n<p>The code 11463 is used to <strong>activate</strong> the controller, to <strong>deactivate</strong> it if necessary the code is 16167.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/regulateur2-6871f13e.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong><br/><br/>3 – Click on ” <strong>Select</strong> “, then click on ” <strong>16 – Multifunction handwheel</strong> “.<br/><br/>4<strong>– </strong>Click on ” <strong>Coding – 07</strong> “. </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/regulateur3-945b80f3.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Options: We note in the penultimate section (0xxxx<strong>?</strong>x):<br/><br/>0 = No regulator installed (CCS not installed)<br/>1 = Regulator installed with 6 positions on a separate switch (CCS with 6 positions …. etc)<br/>3 = Regulator installed with 4 positions on the blinker switch (CCS with 4 positions via Blinker Stalk)</p>\n<p>So replace the “<strong>?</strong>” with the 3 for commodo regulator.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 3:</strong><br/><br/>3 – Click on ” <strong>Select”, then click on ” Select</strong> “. <strong><strong>01 – Engine</strong></strong> ” .<br/><br/>4 – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select byte<strong>“5</strong>“<br/>Check bit<strong>“5</strong>” Cruise Control System (CCS) installed</p>\n<p>Or byte <strong>6</strong> bit <strong>0</strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/regulateur5-1024x491-af7739d1.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-daytime-running-lights-off-drl-with-handbrake-on":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to deactivate the daytime running lights (DRL) when the handbrake is engaged.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>11</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: If this is a hidden bit, replace the last <strong>0</strong> on the right side of the binary number with <strong>a 1</strong>. (<strong>10001000</strong> =&gt; <strong>10001001</strong>)</em></p>","eos-1f-facelift-deactivate-brake-pad-warning-light":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to disable the brake pad wear warning light on the OBD (On-Board Diagnostics) system, for example, for brake pads without a sensor or if the sensor is malfunctioning / Brake Pad Warning Light Deactivation. </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>17 – Instruments</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Cliquer sur « <strong>Codage – 07</strong> » puis cliquer sur « <strong>Assistant Codage Long</strong>« .</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select byte <strong>1</strong>: “<br/>” Then uncheck bit <strong>0: </strong> <strong>“Brakepad warning active/installed”</strong> </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6J0-920-802-J_Module17_Byte1-5e3ec890.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/voyant-frein-d4619ec9.gif\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-deactivate-burnt-out-bulb-display":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Disables the burnt-out bulb display on the ODB. After adding LEDs, your on-board computer may indicate that the replaced bulb is out of order.   <br/><br/>Please note that this depends on your power plant. Save the coding if it doesn’t work to reset it. </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>18</strong>: </em><br/>In binary code, if 0000000 is set to 0, replace with 0010101 so that the value in the byte changes to 15.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-deactivate-esp":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to deactivate ESP completely.  </p>\n<p><strong>ATTENTION: Only valid for vehicles from 2012.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“ABS Brake 03</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Change the value of byte 6, replace <strong>00</strong> by <strong>80</strong><br/>Change the value of byte 14, replace <strong>00</strong> by <strong>01 (do not change byte 14 for TDIs)</strong> </p>\n<p>Simply press the ESP button to deactivate traction control (ASR)</p>\n<p>Press and hold the ESP button for 4s to deactivate the ESC as well.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-deactivate-windshield-washer-indicator":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to deactivate the windshield washer indicator on the ODB (on-board computer).  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>17 – Instruments</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select byte <strong>1</strong>:<br/>Then uncheck Bit <strong>2: </strong>“<strong>Washer Fluid warning active/installed</strong>”.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6J0-920-802-J_Module17_Byte1-5e3ec890.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/laveglace-edc83d4f.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-delete-alarm-belt":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To suppress the audible alarm and the warning light on the speedometer when driving without a seatbelt.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>17 – Instruments</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select byte <strong>1</strong>:<br/>Then uncheck Bit <strong>1: </strong>“<strong>Seatbelt warning active/installed</strong>”.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6J0-920-802-J_Module17_Byte1-5e3ec890.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/voyant_ceinture_de_securite-296648a9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-disable-bluetooth-connection-beep":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: Disables the beep when a device successfully connects to the car radio’s Bluetooth.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“77 – Telephone</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select “Bluetooth acknowledgement signal” from the value list</p>\n<p><strong>6 </strong>– Select <strong>“Off” </strong>from the second drop-down list</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/7P6-035-730-C_Module77_Adaptation_Bluetooth_acknow-580a5d47.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-disable-plate-light-control-odb-led-error":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Disables license plate light control. Useful when installing LED bulbs without resistance or for error prevention.   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>23</strong>:  </p>\n<p>Bit <strong>6</strong>: Uncheck: “Lamp Monitoring (Cold) License Plate Lamps Active”.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/led_plaque5-odt_147-d36567c5.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-door-lighting-retrofit":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables door lighting to be activated after retrofitting.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 (3-door):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>42 – Driver’s door</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – <em>Add <strong>64</strong> to the existing coding.</em></p>\n<p>Repeat for <strong>“52 – Passenger door”</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Connection :  </strong></p>\n<p>Cables must be connected to the 32-pin B connector on the front doors and the 18-pin connector on the rear doors.</p>\n<p>Front doors :</p>\n<p>Pin 18 → Red [+12V] / PIN 2 (harness) to PIN 18 (Connector B)<br/>Pin 19 → Brown [- Ground] / PIN 1 (harness) to PIN 19 (Connector B)</p>\n<p>Rear doors :</p>\n<p>PIN 2 (harness) to PIN 14 (Connector B)<br/>PIN 1 (harness) to PIN 4 (Connector B)</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw-golf-scirocco-door-electronics-module-9f4c48e6.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/door-lights-retfofit-coding-vw-scirocco-golf-6-1392861d.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-door-lock-unlock-confirmation-beep":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: Enables </strong>confirmation of vehicle locking/unlocking via beeps (in conjunction with the alarm) and adds the beep selection menu to the ODB (MFA).  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition switched off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><strong>6 – </strong> Select Byte <strong>5</strong>:</p>\n<p>Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Locking Confirmation via Horn Active”<br/>Bit <strong>4: </strong> Check “Unlocking Confirmation via Horn Active”</p>\n<p>To add the beep selection menu to the ODB (MFA):</p>\n<p>Bit <strong>2</strong>: Disable “<em> </em>” “<em>Horn Confirmation Adaptation Blocked (Adaptation Channel 010/011 &amp; MFA+/FIS)</em>“</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/horng6-2ffc7685.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/horng62-14ab4a90.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-electronic-differential-lock-xds":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the electronic differential lock (XDS).  </p>\n<p>The electronic differential lock (XDS) is an extension of the well-known EDS function. However, XDS does not react to acceleration slip, but rather relieves pressure on the inside front wheel when cornering. XDS applies pressure to the inside wheel during cornering from the hydraulic ESP to prevent slippage.   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>03 – ABS</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>” .</p>\n<p><em>Byte 17 : </em><br/><em>Bit 3: Tick “(Extended) Electronic Differential Lock (XDS – PR-UG3/UG4) active/installed”</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2 XDS setting.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>03 – ABS</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10 </strong>”.</p>\n<p>Channel 36 (Electronic Differential Lock (XDS) in drop-down list)</p>\n<p><em>Choose a value</em><br/> 0<em>“medium” </em><br/>1 “low” <br/>2 “high  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/xdsg6-567d8998.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-enable-bluetooth-retrofit":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables Bluetooth installation for the RCD 510 / RNS 510 after retrofitting the module under the passenger seat.  </p>\n<p>Two different modules are available: Standard (just call) and Premium (call and AD2P music).  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Turn on the ignition, then launch the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 (Enabling Bluetooth in the Gateway):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“19-Gateway CAN</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Installation List</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – <em>Check ” <em>77- Telephone</em></em></p>\n<p><em>Click on “Save coding</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2 (French selection) :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“77 – Telephone</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte <strong>0</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>0-7</strong>: Select “03 Default Language: French ” “.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 3 (Enable Bluetooth Audio (AD2P)):</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on <strong>“77 – Telephone</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 4</strong></p>\n<p><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Uncheck “Multimedia Function (Bluetooth Audio) inactive”.</em></p>\n<p>Switch the ignition off and on again. Bluetooth is activated in the ODB. Enter password (“0000” by default)<br/>Activate Bluetooth on your phone and search the ODB; your phone will appear on the ODB<br/>Start connection with your phone and accept pairing.    </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns510-1024x821-d0438887.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-flash-mode-lights-during-emergency-braking":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: activates the brake/turn signal lights in flash mode during emergency braking.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket. <br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>16</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Emergency Brake Flashing via Brake Light active” (to activate brake lights)</em><br/><em>Bit <strong>2</strong>: Check “Emergency Brake Flashing via Turn Signal active” (to activate turn signals)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/emergencybreakg6-7e26fa37.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-fog-lamps-with-high-beam":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> This feature prevents the fog lights from turning off when you turn on your high beams; they will not turn off when you turn on your high beams.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte<strong> 14</strong>: Clear Bit <strong>2</strong><em> </em>: “<em>Fog lights inactive while high beams are on</em>“</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/antibrouillardpleinphareg6-b0c5522b.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-heated-seat-memory":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>How it works :</strong> When you switch on the heated seat(s), as soon as you remove the key from the ignition the setting is immediately switched off. With this activation, when you switch the ignition back on, the seat will have returned to the level at which you left it.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“08 – Climatronic</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose  </p>\n<p>“<strong>Retention of driver’s seat heater level</strong>” for <strong>Driver’s seat heater memory</strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/3AA-907-044-AG_Module08_Adaptation_retention_of_dr-51646659.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>and/or<strong>“Retention of passenger’s seat heater level</strong>” for <strong>Passenger seat heater memory</strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/3AA-907-044-AG_Module08_Adaptation_retention_of_pa-61b11e06.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><br/><strong>6</strong> – On the same page, there’s a second drop-down menu at the bottom<br/>of the page.<br/>Select<strong>“active</strong>” and confirm.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-hill-hold-control":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: Enables </strong>Hill Hold Control (Hill Start Assist).  </p>\n<p>Hill Hold Control maintains brake pressure for a few seconds after the brake pedal is held down for an extended period.</p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>03 – ABS Brakes</strong>” (or “<strong>03 – ABS Brakes” </strong>).<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>,” then “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>“</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>16: </strong></em><br/><em>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Check “Hill Hold Control (HHC/PR-UG1/UG4) active/installed”</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2: Hill Hold Control Settings.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>03 – ABS Brakes</strong>” (or “<strong>03 – ABS Brakes” </strong>).<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p>Select <strong>Channel 58</strong> (Hill Hold Control (HHC) from the drop-down list)</p>\n<p>Select a value:<br/>0 “normal”<br/>1 “earlier” (restarts with lower acceleration)<br/>2 “later” (restarts with higher acceleration)</p>","eos-1f-facelift-leds-dimmed-with-turn-signals":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Maintains the brightness of the LEDs when the turn signals are on. </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Bytes 15: Bit 3: Check “Daytime Running Light (DRL) inactive with turn signal active”.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-lock-the-cd-dvd-eject-button-on-the-rns-510":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: locks/unlocks the RNS 510’s CD/DVD eject button.  </p>\n<p><em>Protection against theft of your navigation DVD.  </em></p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>Select</strong> ” and run the VCDS program. <strong><strong>37 – Navigation</strong></strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <em><strong>Channel 43</strong> (Ejection Lock)</em> then set the value to <strong>1</strong> <em>(CD/DVD Ejection Lock active)</em></p>\n<p><em>Restart the RNS 510.</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns510-1024x821-d0438887.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-lowering-the-passenger-mirror-when-reversing":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> automatically lowers the passenger mirror when reversing.  </p>\n<p>The mirror adjustment knob must be in position<strong>“R”</strong> for the passenger mirror to lower automatically.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>09 – Centrale Elec</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>” .</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/retrogolf61-d4a12d4d.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/retrogolf62-a22a3a65.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – In the window that appears, select byte<strong> 4</strong> and check bit <strong>3 “</strong> <strong>Mirror Lowering With Reverse Rear </strong>” to activate mirror lowering when shifting into reverse gear:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/retrogolf63-d65eea5e.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>6 </strong>– After this operation, click on the “Output” menu, then validate “OK”.  </p>\n<p>A message appears to inform you that the coding has been accepted.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 2:  </strong></p>\n<p><strong>3 –</strong> Click on<strong> “Select” </strong>, then click on<strong> “52 – Passenger door”.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><em>Add <strong>256</strong> to the value</em></p>","eos-1f-facelift-maintain-led-intensity-with-turn-indicators":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to dim the LEDs with the indicators.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on<strong> “Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 15 </strong>:  </p>\n<p>Bit<strong> 3</strong>: Uncheck “<em>Daytime Running Light (DRL) inactive with turn signal active</em>”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/drlclignotantg6-2853b21b.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-modify-power-steering-hardness":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to adjust the hardness of the power steering for a softer or harder steering wheel.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“44 – Direction assistée” / “44-steering assist.</strong><br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select<strong> Channel 1 </strong>, <em>set a value between<strong> 1</strong> and <strong>15</strong> depending on your hardness preference</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Or Line 2: (If channel 1 is unavailable)</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“44 – Direction assistée” / “44-steering assist.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>xxx1x for smooth steering</li>\n<li>xxx2x for sports management</li>\n<li>xxx3x for hard steering</li>\n<li>xxx4x for harder steering</li>\n<li>xxx5x for very hard steering</li>\n</ul>","eos-1f-facelift-mute-push-to-talk-button":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: Allows you to change the Push-To-Talk (PTT) button on the bottom left of your multifunction steering wheel, to act as a mute button for sound volume.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/></p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>16 – Multifunction Steering Wheel.”</strong><br/></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding- 07</strong>”. then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select <em>Byte<strong> 1</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Uncheck </em><em>“Multi-Function-Steering-Wheel (MFSW) with Push-To-Talk (PPT) Button installed</em>”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pttg6-da3300eb.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>The Push-to-Talk (PPT) button now acts as a mute button.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-needle-check":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the scanning of the speedometer needles when the car is started. When you start your Golf, the speedometer needles move back and forth from minimum to maximum. Style Golf GTI/R. (Needle Sweep)  </p>\n<p><em>Information </em>: If your meter is not compatible, the channel is inactive. Compatible with models after the end of 2009.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>17 – Instruments</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>10-Adaptation</strong>” then in the window that appears, click on the drop-down menu to choose a channel and select the “<strong>Staging</strong>” channel, if unavailable select the “<strong>indicator_celebration</strong>” channel:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/checkg6-9456b738.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – In the “<strong>New value</strong>” box, click on the drop-down menu and select “<strong>active</strong>”. Once this new value has been entered, click on “<strong>Save</strong>”:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/checkg62-c8918051.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-oil-temperature-on-odb":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the Oil Temperature Display in your Grand ODB.  </p>\n<p>It sometimes happens that certain cars don’t have this sensor available in the ODB, including certain Golf MK6s, whether TDI or TSI, and even R versions. Here’s a short tutorial to help you activate it and make it available in your ODB.  </p>\n<p><em>ATTENTION: Modification cannot be activated on all counters.</em> </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>17 – Instruments</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>10-Adaptation</strong>”, then in the window that appears, click on the drop-down menu and select this byte: <strong><em>(7) WIV : Service interval coding Oil level and temperature transmitter</em></strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Vag-Com_01_Sonde_Temperature-d-huile-Golf6R-TSI-TD-913f0cb0.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>English version: Coding For Service Interval Extension TOG</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Vag-Com_01_Sonde_Temperature-d-huile-Golf6R-TSI-TD-6650dcd4.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> <em>Select ”  <strong>Oil level/temp. transmitter connected to instrument holder   </strong>“in the second drop-down list</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/1Z0-920-843-G_Module17_Adaptation_7-WIV_v2-73e7e7f3.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_temp_huile_odb-b53aaa02.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-opening-closing-windows-with-remote-control":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to activate closing and opening of the windows and sunroof with the remote control <strong>WITHOUT </strong>remaining pressed until the windows are completely closed.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 (Pre-2010):</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click <strong>“Select</strong>,” then click <strong>“46 – Central Conv</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click <strong>“Encoding 07</strong>,” then “Long Coding Helper”</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> <em>Byte <strong>6</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>6</strong>: Check “Comfort Function automatic Closing”.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uncheck “Comfort function power windows/sunroof via remote control inactive”.</li>\n<li>Check “Comfort opening power windows via remote control active”.</li>\n<li>Check “Comfort closing power windows via remote control active”.</li>\n<li>Check “Closing sunroof via remote control active” (sunroof)</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/ComfortConv-byte6-9f2c129f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><br/><em>Pressing the remote control’s close button for 3 seconds will first lock the doors and then close the windows, without having to keep the button pressed until they are fully closed</em>.</p>\n<hr/>\n<p><br/><strong>Line 1 (After 2010) :</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click <strong>“Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong><strong>09 – Cent. Elect. </strong></strong> “.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click <strong>“Encoding 07</strong>,” then “Long Coding Helper”</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> <em>Byte <strong>3</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>5</strong>: Check “<em>Comfort Operation via Remote Control Active</em>”.</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/IMG_7790-dfc7ad77.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>Bit <strong>0</strong> and <strong>1</strong> – <em>Comfort Opening/Closing for Sliding Roof Active</em> also opens and closes the sunroof via remote control.</p>","eos-1f-facelift-ops-speed-camera-display-on-car-radio-screen":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables the display of OPS speed cameras on the car radio screen.  </p>\n<p><strong><em>Please note: The vehicle must be equipped with a compatible car radio (RCD-310, RCD-510, RNS-315, RNS-510).</em></strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“10-Station assistance</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Security Access – 16</strong>,” then enter the code “<strong>71679</strong>“</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding- 07</strong>”. then click on<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>6 – </strong>Select <em>Byte<strong> 0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>4</strong>: Tick “Optical illustration active”.</em></p>","eos-1f-facelift-permanent-headlamp-deactivation-scandinavian":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose: Allows you </strong>to disable the low beams that are always on; they are activated only when set to “Auto,” and during the day, only the parking lights are on. (Disable Scandinavian Mode)   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>2008 à 2011:</em></p>\n<p>Uncheck Byte <strong>0</strong> Bit <strong>3</strong></p>\n<p><em>From 2011:</em></p>\n<p>Byte <strong>11</strong> Bit <strong>2</strong>: Uncheck “Permanent Running Light (via Low Beam) active”.  </p>","eos-1f-facelift-programming-a-remote-control":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to program the remote controls that will be used with the vehicle for central locking and unlocking.  </p>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>WARNING:</strong> This procedure does not allow you to program remote controls with the vehicle’s immobilizer.<br/>If your vehicle is equipped with a <strong>KESSY</strong> remote control <strong>(05 – Access/Ignition Authorization)</strong>, you cannot program a new remote control using this procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click <strong>“Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong><strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 01 ( </strong>“Remote Control Adaptation” from the drop-down list)</p>\n<p>Enter the number of keys to program, then click <strong>Save</strong>.<br/>Next, press the <strong>Unlock</strong> button on each remote control to be programmed for at least 1 second. The flashing lights will confirm the process.<br/>All keys must be programmed in a single session, one after the other.<br/>Programming all keys must not take longer than <strong>15 seconds</strong>.<br/>You can add up to <strong>7 remote controls</strong>. If the <strong>[Test]</strong> and/or <strong>[Save]</strong> buttons are grayed out and unclickable, the module likely has all 7 slots filled; delete the remote controls using the <strong>“Reset Remote Controls”</strong> tutorial.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/programmertelecommande-51bf4068.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-resetting-the-remote-controls":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Resets the settings for all remote controls associated with the vehicle. </p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ WARNING:</strong> After completing this procedure, all remote controls will be deleted and will need to be reprogrammed using the <strong>“Program a Remote Control”</strong> tutorial <strong>.</strong><br/>If your vehicle is equipped with a <strong>KESSY</strong> remote control <strong>(05 – Access/Start Authorization)</strong>, you cannot delete the remote controls using this procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click <strong>“Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong><strong><strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></strong></strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 00</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set the value to <strong>0</strong> and save</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6Q0-959-433-E_Module46_Adaptation_Channel00-caced031.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-selective-door-unlocking":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to configure selective door unlocking.  </p>\n<p>When unlocking the vehicle, only the driver’s door is unlocked. To unlock all doors, press the remote control’s unlock button twice.   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Channel 03 (drop-down list: Selective (Single) Door Locking) <em>Set the value to 1</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2 If the adaptation channel is not enabled.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “<strong>Select</strong>,” then click “<strong>09 – Power Plant.</strong>“</p>\n<p><strong>4 – </strong> Click “<strong>Encoding – 07</strong>,” then “<strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong></p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit<strong> 3: </strong> Check “Selective Central Locking active (Single Door Locking)” </em> </p>","eos-1f-facelift-sound-reduction-on-ops-activation":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Automatically lowers speaker volume when OPS is activated.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“10-Station assistance</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>6 – </strong>Select <em>Channel<strong> 08</strong> (Audio Volume Lowering): </em><br/><em>Set value to<strong> 0</strong></em></p>","eos-1f-facelift-speed-limit-display-rns-510":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to display the speed limit on the RNS 510 navigation screen.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“37 – Navigation</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”. Then “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select Byte<strong> 7</strong><br/>Activate Bit <strong>2</strong>: Speed Threshold for Destination Input active</p>\n<p>+ use voice command to activate Bit 3: Speed Threshold for Speech Destination Input active</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/2B758E5F-ACB4-418C-BBBB-B61B0F3A298B-186113f1.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/rns510speed-9dcbaa67.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-start-stop-deactivation":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to disable Start and Stop completely.  <br/><br/><br/><strong><em>Only compatible with Gateway 7N0! Temperature deactivation method. </em></strong> <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“19 – CAN Gateway</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on <strong>“Long Adaptation – 0A”</strong>.<br/><br/><strong>5</strong> – Select Channel<strong>“13</strong><br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – In new value, select<strong>“D02000</strong>” (default value = <strong>002000</strong>) then validate.<br/></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/gof6startstop-1024x528-699ec970.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-tear-wiping":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the windshield wipers once more after using the windshield washer, in order to remove any remaining water from the windshield.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/>3 – Click on ” <strong>Select</strong> “, then click on ” <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” . Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program. 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on <strong>“Coding – 07”</strong> then <strong>“Long Coding Helper”.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select byte <strong>1</strong>, <br/>Check Bit <strong>3</strong></p>","eos-1f-facelift-activate-daytime-running-lights-with-fog-lamps-drl":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Allows fog lights to be used as daytime running lights.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong>: <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1</strong><em> </em>:</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select module “0” and click “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> <em>Byte <strong>14: </strong> </em><br/>Check <em>Bit <strong>1</strong> ” </em><em>Daytime Running Lights (DRL) via Fogs Active</em>“</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/drlantibrouillardg6-df7e4302.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>For the Golf 6 equipped with <strong>xenon</strong> headlights:  </p>\n<p>Byte<strong> 11</strong>: Set Bit<em> </em><strong>2</strong><em> </em>, “<em>Permanent Running Light (PRL) via Low Beam Active</em>“</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/drlantibrouillardxenong6-9278b75a.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>For the Golf 6 equipped with <strong>halogen</strong> headlights:</p>\n<p>Byte<strong> 15</strong>: Set Bit <strong>6</strong> to 0 <strong><em> </em>, “</strong><em>Daytime Running Light (DRL) via separate lights active</em>“</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/drlantibrouillardhalogeneg6-40a8eb5c.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-facelift-window-closure-after-door-opening":"<figure><img alt=\"VAG Coding VCDS ODBeleven VW Eos 1F facelift\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_eos_1f_facelift-791eb94f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Closes windows and sunroof after removing key and opening door.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select</strong>” then click on “<strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> Choose <em>Byte<strong> 4</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>4</strong>: Uncheck “Power Windows/Sunroof </em>disabled after door opening<em>” </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>7: </strong> Check “Power Windows/Sunroof enabled after Terminal 15/Ignition OFF </em>”  </p>","eos-1f-activate-door-bottom-lights-retrofit":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables the activation of the door-bottom lights after a retrofit installation.  </p>\n<p>Connection: Pin 18 and 19 of the 32-pin pin on the driver and front passenger door.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“42-Driver door</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>5 </strong>– Add <strong>+64</strong> to the value</p>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“52 – Passenger door</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> Add <strong>+64</strong> to the value</p>","eos-1f-activate-leaving-home-without-light-sensor":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: automatically switches on the headlights each time the vehicle is unlocked.  </p>\n<p>The headlights will come on whether it’s day or night. They stay on for about 30 sec.   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/>3 – Click on ” <strong>Select</strong> “, then click on ” <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” . Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program. 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click ” <strong>Encoding 07</strong>,” then “Long Coding Helper”</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> <em>Byte <strong>0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit<strong> 5</strong>: Check “Assistance Driving Light Leaving Home active”.</em></p>","eos-1f-activate-mood-lighting":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate ambient lighting.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong>: <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Turn on the ignition, then launch the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then<strong>“Long Coding Helper</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <em>Byte<strong> 20</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Footwell Lights installed”.</em></p>\n<p>To switch on the footlights at the same time as the headlights :</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>16</strong>, check bit<strong> 7</strong> “<em>Ambient Lighting via Footwell Lighting Active</em>”.</p>","eos-1f-activate-turn-signals-in-us-mode":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Enables the US turn signals.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select module “0” and click “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><strong>Byte 7</strong>: Enter value<strong> 32</strong> in <strong>Dec</strong> box<br/>(value in % of intensity, origin: 0)</p>\n<p>Decimal examples shown in yellow in the image:<br/>14 (corresponds to a 20% variation in the indicator lights)<br/>1E (corresponds to a 30% variation in the indicator lights)<br/>28 (corresponds to a 40% variation in the indicator lights)</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/3C0-937-049-Q_Module09_Byte7-16502f21.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-corner-lights-activation":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Activating the <strong>turn signals</strong> causes the fog light on the side toward which you’re turning the steering wheel to turn on when traveling at speeds below 50 km/h. This feature is also known as “cornering lights.” Once this feature is activated on your Golf MKV or Golf MKVI, the fog light on the side of the turn will turn on! It’s super handy on narrow roads or in roundabouts.     </p>\n<p><strong>This modification is only available for vehicles equipped with a 30-byte power unit.</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition switched off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>23</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>2</strong>: Check “Internal Turn Light Control Algorithm active”</em><br/><em>Bits <strong>3–4</strong>: Select “08 Turn Lights via fog lights” from the drop-down list</em></p>","eos-1f-ambient-light-intensity":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Used to adjust ambient lighting intensity.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong>: <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select</strong>” then click on “<strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 05</strong> (Footwell Lightning Dimming)  </p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> Select intensity from 20 to 100% (in 1% increments)</p>","eos-1f-automatic-closing-of-windows-and-roof-in-case-of-rain":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Automatically closes the windows and sunroof when it rains. </p>\n<p>The vehicle must be locked using the remote control for this to work. </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/>3 – Click on ” <strong>Select</strong> “, then click on ” <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” . Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program. 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vitregolf561-c90da3e5.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select module “2” and click ” <strong>Long Coding Helper</strong> “:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vitregolf56-9439cbb9.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – In the window that appears, select byte <strong>0 and </strong>check bit<strong> 2</strong>, <strong>“Rain Closing active</strong>,” to enable the windows and roof to close when it rains:</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Vag-Com-Fermeture-Automatique-des-vitres-5-d61c5dd9.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>7 – After completing this step, click on the “Exit” menu</p>\n<p> Then click “OK”</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Vag-Com-Fermeture-Automatique-des-vitres-7-353fb01b.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click <strong>“Select</strong>,” then click <strong>“46 – Central Conv”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> Then click <strong>” <strong>Long Coding Helper</strong> “</strong></p>\n<p><em><strong>6</strong> – Select Byte <strong>9</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Rain Closing (0 = nonrecurring / 1 = permanent)” </em><br/><em>Bit<strong> 2</strong>: Uncheck “Comfort Closing via Rain Light Sensor (Rain Closing) inactive” </em> </p>","eos-1f-automatic-door-locks":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set automatic door locking from 15km/h.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>Channel 04</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set value to 1</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Verrouillage-automatique-des-portieres_vw-auto-loc-ce03b1da.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte<strong> 0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>4</strong>: Check “Auto-Lock (automatic locking from 15 km/h)”. </em> </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Verrouillage-automatique-des-portieres-1K0_Module4-be0b33ff.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-automatic-door-unlocking":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set automatic door unlocking when the ignition key is removed.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>Canal 05</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set value to 1</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw-door-unlock-798a1163.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p><strong><em>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</em></strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte<strong> 0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>3 : </strong> Check “Auto-Unlock (unlocking after removing key from ignition)”. </em> </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Verrouillage-automatique-des-portieres-1K0_Module4-be0b33ff.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-automatic-headlight-activation-from-140-km-h":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to automatically switch on the lights when the vehicle is travelling at over 140 km/h. </p>\n<p>(The lights will automatically go out if the vehicle is travelling at less than 60 km/h) </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “2 EPIRBs” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte 0 Bit 0: Check “Highway Light Function active above (140kph/90mph)”. </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Calc09-RLS-140kmhBit0-Octet0-4f74e4e9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-automatic-headlight-activation-in-case-of-rain":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to automatically turn on the lights when it’s raining and the wipers have been triggered.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “2 EPIRBs” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>0</strong> Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Rain Light Function Active”.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Calc09-RLS-140kmhBit0-Octet0-4f74e4e9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-comfort-flasher":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set the number of flashes for the “Comfort flashing” option.  <br/><br/><br/><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4 – </strong> Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>.”</p>\n<p><strong>5 </strong>– Select<strong>“Comfort Turn Signal”</strong> from the drop-down list </p>\n<p>Choose a value between 1 and 5 for the number of flashes</p>","eos-1f-coming-home-time-setting":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Set the length of time the headlights remain on after the car is locked.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on <strong>“Adaptation – 10”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 01</strong> (Coming-Home Time)  </p>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – Select duration from <strong>0 to 60 sec</strong> (in 1sec increments).</p>","eos-1f-correction-of-odb-consumption-display":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to correct the fuel consumption display on the on-board computer.  </p>\n<p><strong>Modification only possible on Mid/High range models</strong>. <br/><em>Note: modifications affect the display of instantaneous consumption, average consumption and fuel quantity.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on “<strong>Select</strong>”, then click on <strong>“17 – Instruments”</strong>. <strong>Procedure:</strong> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation- 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 03</strong> <em>“Consumption Correction”.</em></p>\n<p><em>Adjust consumption correction factor between 85% and 115%.</em> <em>(base value: 100)</em></p>\n<p><em>Example 1: Calculation based on volume per distance (e.g. l/100 km): DIS: 9.3 l/100 km. Actual: 9.8 l/100 km. (100, 9,8 / 9,73 ? 105,38) Channel 3 will be changed from 100 to 105 <br/>Example 2: Calculation based on volume distance (e.g. MPG is: DIS: 23 MPG. Actual: 24 MPG. (100, 23/24 and 95.83) Channel 3 will be changed from 100 to 96</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/correction-consommation-odb-d51db18e.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-cruise-control-activation-retrofit":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to activate the speed governor after retrofitting.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>01 – Motor</strong> “.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on ” <strong><strong>Access</strong></strong> – <strong> 11</strong> ” then enter the code ” <strong><em><strong>11463</strong></em></strong> ” </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/regulateur2-6871f13e.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>16 – Steering Wheel</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Coding – 07</strong> ” then </p>\n<p><em>The penultimate digit (0xxxx<strong>?</strong>x) is used to configure the cruise control.</em><br/><em>Enter <strong>2</strong> if you don’t have an on-board computer</em><br/><em>Set <strong>4</strong> if you have an on-board computer</em></p>\n<p>.</p>","eos-1f-deactivate-brake-pad-warning-light":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong> Allows you to disable the brake pad wear warning light on the OBD (On-Board Diagnostics) system, for example, for brake pads without a sensor or if the sensor is malfunctioning / Brake Pad Warning Light Deactivation. </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>17 – Instruments</strong>.”<br/><br/><strong>4 – </strong>Click on <strong>“Coding –</strong> 07”  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> <em>–  <em>The third and fourth digits of the code (xx<strong>??</strong>x0x) are used to change the settings for the Instrument module. To turn off the brake pad warning light, subtract <strong>01</strong> from these digits. </em></em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/17-Instruments-Coding-Golf5-2c5d8d07.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>In the example above, the initial code is: <strong>0027103</strong></p>\n<p>To turn off the brake pad warning light, simply recode it as follows: <strong>0026103 </strong><em>(27 – 01 = 26)</em></p>\n<p>To turn on the brake pad warning light: +01</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/voyant-frein-d4619ec9.gif\"/></figure>","eos-1f-deactivate-burnt-out-bulb-display":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>To deactivate the burnt-out bulb indicator on the ODB. After adding LEDs, your on-board computer may indicate that the replaced bulb is out of order.   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>18</strong> Bit<strong> 0</strong>: Uncheck “Cold Diagnosis License Plate Lights active” for plates</p>\n<p>the other bits for the other bulbs of your choice (see image)</p>\n<p>Byte 19 Bit 1: uncheck “Cold Diagnosis Front Parking Lights active” for night lights</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/IMG_6231-0119a9fa.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>Or for all bulbs:</p>\n<p>Byte 2 Bit 2: Cold Lamp Diagnosis Active</p>\n<p><br/>Eliminate burnt-out bulb errors after coding.</p>","eos-1f-deactivate-windshield-washer-indicator":"<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to deactivate the windshield washer indicator on the ODB (on-board computer).  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>17 – Instruments</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> <em>–  <em>The third and fourth coding digits (xx<strong>??</strong>x0x) are used to modify Instrument module options. To remove the ice-washer indicator, subtract <strong>04</strong> from these digits. </em></em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/17-Instruments-Coding-Golf5-2c5d8d07.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>In the example above, the initial coding is: <strong>0027103</strong></p>\n<p>To remove the windscreen washer indicator, simply recode as follows: <strong>0023103 </strong><em>(27 – 04 = 23)</em></p>\n<p>To activate the ice washer indicator: +04</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/laveglace-edc83d4f.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-defrost-time-setting":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set the operating time for mirror and rear window defrosting.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong> then click on “<strong>09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 03</strong> (Rear Window &amp; Outside Mirror Heater Activation Time)  </p>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – Select duration from <strong>1 to 254 min</strong> (in 1 min increments).</p>","eos-1f-delete-belt-alarm":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: To suppress the audible alarm and the warning light on the speedometer when driving without a seatbelt.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition, then run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/> 3 – Click on “<strong>Select</strong>”, then click on “<strong>17 – Instruments</strong>”. 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> –  <em>The third and fourth coding digits (xx<strong>??</strong>x0x) are used to modify Instrument module options. To suppress the belt alarm, subtract <strong>2</strong> from these digits. </em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/17-Instruments-Coding-Golf5-2c5d8d07.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>In the example above, the initial coding (with belt alarm) is: <strong>0023103</strong></p>\n<p>To delete the belt alarm, simply recode as follows: <strong>0021103 </strong><em>(23 – 02 = 21)</em></p>\n<p>To activate the belt alarm: +02</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/voyant_ceinture_de_securite-296648a9.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-display-speed-on-rcd-300":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> To add a driving-school mode view to the RCD 300 that displays speed (also ideal for accompanied driving) <br/><br/><br/><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket. <br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“56 – Radio</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.<br/><br/><strong>6</strong> – On this page, at the very top, there is a drop-down menu.<br/>In this drop-down menu, choose<strong>“Channel 2</strong>” “Driving School Function”.<br/><br/><strong>7</strong> – Set value to <strong>1</strong>.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/RCD300_Module56_Channel02-3c0994f0.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>To activate the function:<br/>– Switch off the RCD-300<br/>– Press the MENU button<br/>– Keep the MENU button pressed and switch on the RCD-300.</p>\n<p><em>(To return to normal display, execute the same sequence)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vw_rcd300-efa2d2ba.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-door-lock-confirmation-key":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong> Allows you to configure the door lock confirmation when using the key.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 – </strong> Click “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>“</p>\n<p><strong>Channel 11</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set the value to 0 (no confirmation)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 1 (confirmation with turn signals)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 2 (confirmation with beep/horn)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 3 (confirmation with turn signals and a beep/horn)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2: </strong></p>\n<p>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>11</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0: </strong> Check “Locking Confirmation (Blink) via Door Locks” (confirmation with blinking lights)</em><br/><em>Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Locking Confirmation (Horn) via Door Locks” (confirmation with a beep or horn)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/locking_196-096e4b1a.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-door-lock-confirmation-remote-control":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong> Allows you to configure the door lock confirmation when using the remote control. </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>Channel 10</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set the value to 0 (no confirmation)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 1 (confirmation with turn signals)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 2 (confirmation with beep/horn)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 3 (confirmation with turn signals and a beep/horn)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/vagcom-golf5-e5c80be6.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>11</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>2: </strong> Check “Locking Confirmation (Blink) via Remote Control” (confirmation with turn signals)</em><br/><em>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Check “Locking Confirmation (Horn) via Remote Control” (confirmation with a beep or horn)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/locking_196-096e4b1a.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-door-unlock-confirmation-remote-control":"<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to set the door unlock confirmation when using the remote control.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>Channel 07</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set the value to 0 (no confirmation)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 1 (confirmation with turn signals)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 2 (confirmation with beep/horn)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 3 (confirmation with turn signals and a beep/horn)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>10</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit<strong> 2</strong>: Check “Unlocking Confirmation (Blink) via Remote Control” (confirmation with flashing lights)</em><br/><em>Bit <strong>3</strong>: Tick “Unlocking Confirmation (Horn) via Remote Control” (confirmation with Beep/Horn)</em></p>","eos-1f-door-unlocking-confirmation-key":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong> Allows you to configure the door unlock confirmation when using the key.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>Channel 08</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set the value to 0 (no confirmation)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 1 (confirmation with turn signals)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 2 (confirmation with beep/horn)</em><br/><em>Set the value to 3 (confirmation with turn signals and a beep/horn)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>10</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Unlocking Confirmation (Blink) via Door Locks” (confirmation with blinking lights)</em><br/><em>Bit <strong>1</strong>: Check “Unlocking Confirmation (Horn) via Door Locks” (confirmation with a beep or horn)</em></p>","eos-1f-flash-mode-during-emergency-braking":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: activates brake lights in flash mode during emergency braking.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure </strong>: <br/>1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/>2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>17</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Emergency Brake Flash Light active”.</em></p>","eos-1f-fold-down-mirrors-with-ignition-off-and-door-open":"<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: Allows mirrors to be folded down even after the ignition has been switched off and a door opened.  </p>\n<p><em>(Also enables windows and sunroof to be closed)</em> </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1 :</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on “<strong>Select”</strong>, then click on “<strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Coding – 07</strong>” then click on “<strong>Long Coding Helper</strong>” .</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>6</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>7</strong>: Uncheck “Power Windows/Sunroof disabled after door Opening”.</em></p>","eos-1f-headlight-washer-duration-setting":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to set how long the headlight washers remain activated.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on <strong>“Adaptation – 10”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 04</strong> (Headlight Washer Activation Time)  </p>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – Select a duration between <strong>50 and 10,000 ms</strong> (in 50-ms increments).</p>","eos-1f-leaving-home-duration-setting":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> to set the length of time the headlights remain on after the car is unlocked.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on <strong>“Adaptation – 10”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 02</strong> (Leaving-Home Time)  </p>\n<p><strong>6</strong> – Select duration from <strong>0 to 60 sec</strong> (in 1sec increments).</p>","eos-1f-modify-power-steering-hardness":"<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: to adjust the hardness of the power steering for a softer or harder steering wheel.  </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“44 – Direction assistée” / “44-steering assist.</strong><br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Adaptation – 10</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select<strong> Channel 1 </strong>, <em>set a value between<strong> 1</strong> and <strong>15</strong> depending on your hardness preference</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Or Line 2: (If channel 1 is unavailable)</strong>:<br/><br/> 3 – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>” then click on<strong>“44 – Direction assistée” / “44-steering assist.”</strong><br/><br/> 4<strong>– </strong> Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5 – </strong>Select :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>xxx1x for smooth steering</li>\n<li>xxx2x for sports management</li>\n<li>xxx3x for hard steering</li>\n<li>xxx4x for harder steering</li>\n<li>xxx5x for very hard steering</li>\n</ul>","eos-1f-one-touch-window-closing":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to activate closing and opening of the windows and sunroof with the remote control <strong>WITHOUT </strong>remaining pressed until the windows are completely closed.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition turned off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostic port.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Turn on the ignition and launch the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>46 – Central Conv.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click ” <strong>Encoding 07</strong>,” then “Long Coding Helper”</p>\n<p><strong>5 –</strong> <em>Byte <strong>6</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>6</strong>: Check “Comfort Function automatic Closing”.</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Uncheck “Comfort function power windows/sunroof via remote control inactive”.</li>\n<li>Check “Comfort opening power windows via remote control active”.</li>\n<li>Check “Comfort closing power windows via remote control active”.</li>\n<li>Check “Closing sunroof via remote control active” (sunroof)</li>\n</ul>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/ComfortConv-byte6-9f2c129f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>46 – Comfort Module.”</strong><br/><br/><strong>4 – </strong>Click “ <strong>Adaptation – 10”</strong> </p>\n<p><strong>Channel 17</strong> (Comfort function)</p>\n<p><em>Set value to 1</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/IMG_0944-1024x741-ec4d5180.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><em>Pressing the remote control’s close button for 3 seconds will first lock the doors and then close the windows, without having to keep the button pressed until they are fully closed</em>.</p>","eos-1f-permanent-headlight-deactivation-scandinavian":"<p><strong>Purpose: Allows you </strong>to disable the low beams that are always on; they are activated only when set to “Auto,” and during the day, only the parking lights are on. (Disable Scandinavian Mode)   </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “0” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p>Byte <strong>0</strong> Uncheck Bit <strong>3</strong></p>","eos-1f-programming-a-remote-control":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Allows you to program the remote controls that will be used with the vehicle for central locking and unlocking.  </p>\n<p>⚠️ <strong>WARNING:</strong> This procedure does not allow you to program the remote controls with the vehicle’s immobilizer.</p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 01 ( </strong>“Remote Control Adaptation” from the drop-down list)</p>\n<p>Enter the number of keys to program, then click <strong>Save</strong>.<br/>Next, press the <strong>Unlock</strong> button on each remote control to be programmed for at least 1 second. The flashing lights will confirm the procedure.<br/>All keys must be programmed in a single session, one after the other.<br/>Programming all keys must not take longer than <strong>15 seconds</strong>.<br/>You can add up to <strong>4 remote controls</strong>. If the <strong>[Test]</strong> and/or <strong>[Save]</strong> buttons are grayed out and unclickable, the module likely has all 4 slots filled; delete the remote controls using the <strong> “Reset Remote Controls”</strong> tutorial.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6Q0-959-433-E_Module46_Adaptation_Channel01-10148edb.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-reset-drain-interval-to-zero":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: to reset the drain interval.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure:</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong></strong> 2 – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>17 – Instruments.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “<strong>Adaptation- 10</strong>” .</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 02</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set value to <strong>0</strong></em></p>\n<p>To reset the drain interval completely and automatically, follow the steps below.</p>\n<p>Click on “Reset maintenance” on the VCDS home screen.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/accueil_vcds-0c100c7a.webp\"/></figure>\n<p>Wait for the following window to open and select “reset service” from the drop-down list, then click on “Start operation”.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/reset_entretien_vcds-b31903a6.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-resetting-the-remote-controls":"<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> Resets the settings for all remote controls associated with the vehicle.</p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ WARNING:</strong> After completing this procedure, all remote controls will be deleted and will need to be reprogrammed using the <strong>“How to Program a Remote Control”</strong> tutorial <strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select <strong>Channel 00</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set the value to <strong>0</strong> and save</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/6Q0-959-433-E_Module46_Adaptation_Channel00-caced031.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-selective-door-unlocking":"<p><strong>Purpose </strong>: Used to set up selective door unlocking. </p>\n<p>When unlocking the vehicle, only the driver’s door is unlocked. To unlock all doors, press the remote control’s unlock button twice.   </p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong> <br/><strong>1</strong> – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong><br/><br/><strong>3</strong> – Click on ” <strong>Select”</strong>, then click on ” <strong>46 – Comfort Module</strong>“.<br/><br/><strong>4 </strong>– Click on “<strong>Adaptation – 10</strong>”.  </p>\n<p><strong>Channel 03</strong></p>\n<p><em>Set value to 1</em></p>\n<p><strong>Line 2:</strong></p>\n<p><strong><em>If the adaptation channel is not activated.</em></strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select”</strong>, then click on<strong>“46 – Comfort module</strong>“.</p>\n<p><strong>4 </strong>– Click on<strong>“Coding – 07</strong>” then on ” <strong>Long Coding Helper”</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>0</strong>: </em><br/><em>Bit <strong>0</strong>: Check “Selective Central Locking (Single Door Opening)” </em> </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/Verrouillage-automatique-des-portieres-1K0_Module4-be0b33ff.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-set-light-sensor-sensitivity":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>Adjusts the sensitivity of the light sensor.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure: </strong><br/> 1 – Engine off, ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then plug the other end into your vehicle’s diagnostics socket.</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> – Switch on the ignition and run the VCDS program.</p>\n<p><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click on<strong>“Select</strong>”, then click on<strong>“09 – Cent. Elect.</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on<strong>“Coding 07</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select the “2 EPIRBs” module from the drop-down list and click on “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><em>Byte <strong>2</strong>: </em><br/> Bits<em><strong> 0-7</strong></em> (187, 205 or 237 to increase sensitivity, or 45, 40 or 33 to decrease sensitivity)</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/lightsensorg5-1e88d9a5.webp\"/></figure>","eos-1f-show-hidden-menu-climatronic":"<p><strong>Purpose: </strong>to display the Climatronic’s hidden menu.  </p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/menu-cache-climatronic-golf5-7154b4a2.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>Displaying instantaneous digital speed? it’s possible! </strong>  No need to use your VCDS VAG-COM cable for this tutorial.</p>\n<p><strong>Proceed :</strong></p>\n<p>It’s really quite simple. <strong>Just press the “ECON” button on your air conditioner and the “air extraction up” button, symbolized by a person with an arrow pointing upwards, at the same time</strong>.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/climatronic1-5edb6af4.gif\"/></figure>\n<p>Then simply move the driver-side climate control knob to access the various temperatures and functions.</p>\n<p><strong>What’s it for?</strong></p>\n<p>The main things to check are engine temperature, cabin temperature, exterior temperature and digital speed.</p>\n<p>For digital speed:  </p>\n<p>Then set to -19.0 in the left-hand display by turning the left-hand knob, and the right-hand display gives you the “real” speed in km/h.</p>\n<p>This is because the “real” speedometer is different from the analogue speedometer (needle) which has passed 60km/h.</p>\n<p>Above 99km/h, the right-hand display alternates between the 100km/h in the small right-hand corner and the remaining two digits in the large right-hand corner.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/climatronic2-1d05bbc0.gif\"/></figure>\n<p>Turning the central knob changes the small exponent number on the left display, which changes the display on the right to a small 2 – the speed is in mph.</p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/climatronic3-04295505.gif\"/></figure>\n<hr/>\n<p><strong>List of hidden Climatronic functions</strong></p>\n<p><br/>The beginning of each paragraph symbolizes a function (1st line) followed by sub-functions (following lines).</p>\n<p><strong>0</strong> System data (temperature setting left)<br/>0 Program number Dig. (temperature setting right)<br/>1 ERL disaster Dig.<br/>2 ERL right Dig.<br/>3 Working point on/on Dig.<br/>4 Automatic ricircolo (motor)/Automatic ricircolo Bit Dig.<br/>5 Heating as stopped I activate Dig.<br/>6 Air quality<br/>7 Derivative Dig.<br/>8 Country code Dig.<br/>9 Zone sensor test °C </p>\n<p><strong>1</strong> UIF (temperature setting left)<br/>0 Internal temperature °C (temperature setting right)<br/>1 Surface temperature °C<br/>2 Platinum piastrin temperature (?) °C<br/>3 Platinum piastrin corrector (?) °C<br/>4 uif-Sonnenint (?) Dig.<br/>5 Radiation corrector (?) °C<br/>6 Dynamic correctors °C<br/>7 Ricircolo corrector °C<br/>8 Thermal correctors °C<br/>9 UIF ignition compensation °C</p>\n<p><strong>2</strong> Irradiance sensor SX<br/>0 Irradiance not delayed (?) W/m2<br/>1 Irradiance corrector for temperature controller W/m2<br/>2 Irradiance corrector for fan W/m2<br/>3 Value to be digitized.</p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> Irradiance sensor DX<br/>0 Irradiance not delayed (?) W/m2<br/>1 Irradiance correction for temperature controller W/m2<br/>2 Irradiance correction for fan W/m2<br/>3 Value to be digitized.</p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> Outdoor temperature<br/>0 Outdoor temperature °C<br/>1 Temp. Outdoor CAN line °C<br/>2 Intake air temperature climate °C </p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> Theoretical SX temperature<br/>0 Theoretical SX outlet air temp. °C<br/>1 Difference between theoretical and actual temp. °C<br/>2 Value of sx. regulation ½ % /Dig<br/>3 Stell-Max (?) ½ % /Dig<br/>4 Defrost SX    </p>\n<p><strong>6</strong> Noticed temperature SX<br/>0 Temp. Noticed SX °C<br/>1 Temp. Feet SX °C<br/>2 Temp. Frontal SX °C   </p>\n<p><strong>7</strong> Theoretical temperature DX<br/>0 Theoretical air temperature DX °C<br/>1 Difference between theoretical and actual temperature DX °C<br/>2 Control values controller ½ % /Dig<br/>3 Stell-Max (?) ½ % /Dig<br/>4 Defrost DX   </p>\n<p><strong>8</strong> Noticed temperature DX<br/>0 Noticed outlet air temp. DX °C<br/>1 Temp. Feet °C<br/>2 Temp. Frontal °C   </p>\n<p><strong>9</strong> Theoretical evaporator temperature<br/>0 Theoretical evaporator temperature °C<br/>1 Difference between theoretical and effective evaporator temp. °C<br/>2 Evaporator control values 5mA/Dig<br/>3 Evaporator control modes (see below)  </p>\n<p><strong>10</strong> Effective evaporator temperature<br/>0 Effective evaporator temperature °C<br/>1 Refrigerant pressure climate bar<br/>2 Average refrigerant temperature climate °C</p>\n<p><strong>11</strong> Compressore clim<br/>0 Theoretical electric power but<br/>1 Effective electric power but<br/>2 Max electric power but<br/>3 Kompressor PWM (0… 200) Dig<br/>4 Electric power compressore offset<br/>5 Electric power values<br/>6 Code compressore disinserito</p>\n<p><strong>12</strong> Fan climate<br/>0 Theoretical fan voltage V<br/>1 Manual control<br/>2 Fault code<br/>3 Riduction fan V<br/>4 Offset fan (from here to automatic mode) 0.1V</p>\n<p><strong>13</strong> Bocchette air temperature SX<br/>0 Theoretical value formattato %<br/>1 Theoretical value actual temp. Bocchette sx. Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. temp. Bocchette sx. Dig.<br/>3 Limite plus grand temp. Bocchette sx. Dig.<br/>4 Limit min. temp. Bocchette sx. Dig.          </p>\n<p><strong>14</strong> Bocchette air temperature DX<br/>0 Theoretical value formattato %<br/>1 Theoretical value real temp. Bocchette dx Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. temp. Bocchette dx Dig.<br/>3 Limite plus grand temp. Bocchette dx. Dig.<br/>4 Limit min. temp. Bocchette dx. Dig.        </p>\n<p><strong>15</strong> Temperature bocchetta pieds/centrale<br/>0 Theoretical value formattato %<br/>1 Actual theoretical value temp. Bocchetta centrale Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. temp. bocchetta centrale Dig.<br/>3 Limite plus grand temp. bocchetta centrale Dig.<br/>4 Limite min. temp. bocchetta centrale Dig.      </p>\n<p><strong>16</strong> Pelle Defrost<br/>0 Theoretical value formattato %<br/>1 Actual theoretical value pelle Defrost Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. pelle Defrost Dig.<br/>3 Limit plus pelle Defrost Dig.<br/>4 Limit min. pelle Defrost Dig.  </p>\n<p><strong>17</strong> Pelle ricircolo interne<br/>0 Valeur théorique formattato %<br/>1 Valeur théorique réelle pelle ricircolo Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. pelle ricircolo Dig.<br/>3 Limite plus grand pelle ricircolo Dig.<br/>4 Limite min. pelle ricircolo Dig.  </p>\n<p><strong>18</strong> Cold air shovel<br/>0 Theoretical value formattato %<br/>1 Theoretical actual value Cold air shovel Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. Pelle air froid Dig.<br/>3 Limite plus grand Pelle air froid Dig.<br/>4 Limite min. Cold air shovel Dig.  </p>\n<p><strong>19</strong> Engine data<br/>0 Speeds’ km/h<br/>1 Engine temperature °C<br/>2 Engine rpm U/min<br/>3 Compressor rpm climate U/min</p>\n<p><strong>20</strong> Data sent on CAN<br/>0 of Additional heating (on demand)1 = inserted<br/>1 Conferma el. PTC 1 = inserted<br/>2 Puissance el. effective PTC à 3% air demandé radiatore de<br/>clima%<br/>4 Rappo% de pic de l’air radiatore%<br/>5 Aucune prestation demandée au disp. de chauffage 1=nessuna prestation chauffage   </p>\n<p><strong>21</strong> Ripartitore d’air Climat<br/>0 Defrost% 1 Frontale%<br/>2 Piedi%<br/>3 Bocchetta areazione%<br/>4 Ricircolo air frais/propre (+ bocchetta… ?) %<br/>5 Évaluation sensore%</p>\n<p><strong>22</strong> Timer data<br/>0 Stop period min.<br/>1 Motor running time S<br/>2 Running time with key inserted s</p>\n<p><strong>23</strong> Special channel<br/>0 Internal lighting sensor Dig.<br/>1 Air quality sensor</p>\n<p><strong>24</strong> Software version<br/>0 Development status<br/>1 Software version<br/>2 Type SW<br/>3 Keep Frozen<br/>4 Version K Matrix<br/>5 EEP -Testflags<br/>6 Codifica Vag (?) 2</p>\n<p><strong>25</strong> Canali di prova<br/>0 Canale 0<br/>1 Canale1<br/>2 Canale2<br/>3 Canale3<br/>4 Canale4<br/>5 Canale5<br/>6 Canale6<br/>7 Canale7<br/>8 Canale8<br/>9 Canale9</p>\n<p><strong>26</strong> Parameters for readings on crystals<br/>0 I delay s<br/>1 Step/udm (?) digit<br/>2 Limit minimo digit<br/>3 Limit max. digit<br/>4 Step/udm on HellaKit (?) 1=ein </p>\n<p><strong>27</strong> Bocchetta areazione directe<br/>0 Theoretical value formattato%<br/>1 Actual theoretical value Bocchetta areazione directe Dig.<br/>2 Conferma el. Bocchetta areazione directe Dig.<br/>3 Limite max. Bocchetta areazione directe Dig.<br/>4 Limite min. Bocchetta areazione directe Dig.   </p>\n<p><strong>28</strong> Umidita<br/>0 Umidita’22#teorica%<br/>1 Umidita’relative to temperature UIF ? %<br/>2 Temp. WSS crystals °C<br/>3 Temperature of condensation °C<br/>4 Melting point temperature difference °C </p>\n<p><strong>29</strong> Raw values himidity<br/>0 Temp. Crystals, corrected °C<br/>1 Temp.  Crystals, uncorrected °C<br/>2 Sensor umidità%<br/>3 Sensor temperature °C<br/>4 Condensation point temperature (test measurement) °C<br/>5 Fault code</p>\n<p><strong>30</strong> Indicated values you himidity<br/>0 Sensor value termofil oDig.<br/>1 Temperatura rif. termofilo Dig.<br/>2 Periodic duration himidity 10-4 sec<br/>3 Temp. Reference himidity Dig.<br/>4 Himidity Rel. on sensore%<br/>5 Sensor temperature himidity °C<br/>6 Crystal temperature (uncorrected) °C   </p>\n<p><strong>What’s in it for me?</strong></p>\n<p>None, unless you like numbers, and the fact that you can see your speed in real time other than with a needle is very nice.</p>\n<p><strong>How do I get out of this menu?</strong></p>\n<p>By switching off the engine or pressing the “AUTO” and “ECON” buttons simultaneously.</p>","eos-1f-activate-daytime-running-lights-with-fog-lamps-drl":"<p><strong>Purpose</strong>: Allows fog lights to be used as daytime running lights.  </p>\n<p><strong>Procedure</strong>: <br/><strong>1</strong> – With the engine off and the ignition off, connect your official VAG-COM VCDS cable to the computer, then connect the other end to your vehicle’s diagnostic port.<br/><br/><strong>2</strong> – Turn on the ignition, then launch the VCDS program.<br/><br/><strong>Line 1:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>3</strong> – Click “ <strong>Select</strong>,” then click “ <strong>09 – Cent. Elect.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>4</strong> – Click on “ <strong>Code 07.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>5</strong> – Select module “0” and click “Long Coding Helper”:</p>\n<p><strong>6 –</strong> <em>Byte 0: </em><br/><em>Bit 4: Check “Daytime Running Lights (North America)”</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/scandinavie_194-c7df4fa8.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>7 – </strong> <em>Byte 17: </em><br/><em>Bit 4: Check “Daytime Running Light with dimmed Low Beam” (or “Daytime Running Light via Fog Lights,” depending on your version of VCDS)</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/ab_975-e7b60077.webp\"/></figure>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/veilleusejour3-c639151f.webp\"/></figure>\n<p><strong>8-</strong> Adjusting the fog light intensity (from 1% to 99%)</p>\n<p><em>Byte 20: </em><br/><em>Change the value in the Dec box</em></p>\n<figure><img decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/VagComByte20-adce5646.webp\"/></figure>"};
